Here is a buggy JavaScript function. Find the bug and give the fixed code only.

function sum(arr) {
  let total;
  for (let i = 0; i <= arr.length; i++) total += arr[i];
  return total;
}

message.content:
The bug is an off-by-one error in the loop condition. The condition `i <= arr.length` causes the loop to access an index that is out of bounds (`arr[arr.length]` is undefined), which results in `NaN` being returned.

The fix is to change the condition to `i < arr.length`.

```javascript
function sum(arr) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) total += arr[i];
  return total;
}
```
